=== The righteous and the wicked
# from 11239
--- How the fool, the wise and the righteous learn
kicker: The righteous and the wicked
table: Who | How they learn right from wrong
tr: The fool | Never learns from experience. They do the same foolishness over and over again and expect it to work out differently “this time.”
tr: The average person | From their own experience, imperfectly.
tr: The wise | Not only from their own, but by others' experiences.
tr: The righteous | From God's word, and believe Him when He says something is so.
tr: The wicked | Know right from wrong and do it anyway.
tr: The hypocrite | Knows it, and holds it against others who fall short, while failing to maintain it themselves.

# from 17848, 17201, 520
--- The fear of God divides them
kicker: The righteous and the wicked
big: Psalms 36:1 | mark: there is no fear of God before his eyes
p: The contrast between the wicked and righteous is the fear of the Lord. The love of truth and abhorring evil. The prideful versus the humility of falling before God.
p: *A scorner seeketh wisdom, and findeth it not* ([[Proverbs 14:6]]). How can they, without the fear of God? *Ever learning, and never able to come to the knowledge of the truth* ([[2 Timothy 3:7]]). God grants us wisdom by His word and by the rod of reproof ([[Proverbs 1:23]]).

# from 10571, 13082
--- Pride goeth before destruction
kicker: The righteous and the wicked
big: Proverbs 16:18 | mark: Pride goeth before destruction
p: The things God hates are the things we are called to hate, and pride is one of the things God hates most ([[Proverbs 6:16-19]]). It is why the sodomites use the term. They defy God by announcing their pride, against His judgments.
p: *But wisdom is justified of her children* ([[Matthew 11:19]]). What people think to give as their opinion is shown to be true or not by what following it begets. The fruit that wisdom bears.

# from 17184, 17185
--- The wise son
kicker: The righteous and the wicked
big: Proverbs 10:1 | mark: A wise son maketh a glad father
p: God blesses those that prepare, gathering in the summer against the winter. Sleeping through the harvest is a shame ([[Proverbs 10:5]]). The harvest analogy is not just for earthly goals, but the sharing of the gospel. Go soul-winning! He that winneth souls is wise.
p: *The way of the LORD is strength to the upright: but destruction shall be to the workers of iniquity* ([[Proverbs 10:29]]).
read: app:doctrine-wisdom~11239 | How the fool, wise and righteous learn
read: app:doctrine-wisdom~17201 | The scorner seeketh wisdom and findeth it not
read: app:doctrine-wisdom~10571 | Pride goes before destruction

=== The tongue
# from 17235, 17206
--- Death and life
kicker: The tongue
big: Proverbs 18:21 | mark: Death and life are in the power of the tongue
p: Imagine every foolish or wicked thing you've ever said in anger, read aloud by the holy God for all to hear ([[Matthew 12:36]]). I thank God for His great salvation, that spares me from such utter humiliation.
p: *A soft answer turneth away wrath* ([[Proverbs 15:1]]). *A word spoken in due season, how good is it!* ([[Proverbs 15:23]]) How good it is to hear the joy of a brother when we are feeling down!

# from 17863, 17403
--- A bridle on the mouth
kicker: The tongue
big: Psalms 39:1 | mark: I will keep my mouth with a bridle
p: Parents with adult children, or pastors with a flock, should recognize this feeling. When we see people we love being led away, and we know if we speak out in anger, that we'd do more harm than good. *My heart was hot within me* ([[Psalms 39:3]]).
p: *A fool uttereth all his mind: but a wise man keepeth it in till afterwards* ([[Proverbs 29:11]]). Blabbing every idle thought that comes into your head is vain ([[James 3:2-5]]).

# from 17330, 17207
--- Talebearers and evil surmising
kicker: The tongue
big: Proverbs 26:20 | mark: where there is no talebearer, the strife ceaseth
p: Evil surmising is harboring unproven, malicious suspicions, assuming the worst motives about others without evidence ([[1 Timothy 6:4-5]]). They take innocent actions and invent malicious intent, spreading rumors to poison others. Don't be one, but also don't fall victim to one by listening to their talebearing.
p: The words we say are a window to our heart ([[Matthew 12:34]]). *The eyes of the LORD are in every place, beholding the evil and the good* ([[Proverbs 15:3]]).
read: app:doctrine-wisdom~17235 | Death and life in the power of the tongue
read: app:doctrine-wisdom~17330 | Talebearers, evil surmising and discord

=== Wisdom and the strange woman
# from 17166, 17172, 17182
--- The strange woman
kicker: The strange woman
big: Proverbs 5:3-5 | mark: her end is bitter as wormwood
p: The analogy of adultery serves as both a metaphor and practical application. The strange woman of folly speaks sweet sayings, but her end is bitter. God's paths are straight and knowable, but her ways are moveable. Sin will take you further than you want to go, keep you longer than you want to stay, and cost you more than you want to pay.
p: She says the goodman is gone a long journey ([[Proverbs 7:19-20]]). There is an allegory here: the temptation that judgment of God is afar off. *Stolen waters are sweet* ([[Proverbs 9:17]]). But her guests are in the depths of Hell.

# from 17166, 17430
--- The wife of thy youth
kicker: The strange woman
big: Proverbs 5:18 | mark: rejoice with the wife of thy youth
p: *King Solomon loved many strange women... and his wives turned away his heart* ([[1 Kings 11:1-4]]). The wisest man in history fell prey to the very thing he counseled against. It is often true that the preacher preaches what he needs to hear, more than the audience.
p: *Give not thy strength unto women, nor thy ways to that which destroyeth kings* ([[Proverbs 31:3]]). Women were not just problems Solomon carried, but a downfall for his father David, as well.
read: app:doctrine-wisdom~17166 | The strange woman and the wife of thy youth
read: app:doctrine-wisdom~17172 | The harlot's snare and the goodman gone

=== True riches
# from 17985, 17188
--- Riches profit not
kicker: True riches
big: Proverbs 11:4 | mark: Riches profit not in the day of wrath: but righteousness delivereth from death
p: Wise men die, likewise the fool and the brutish person perish, and leave their wealth to others ([[Psalms 49:10]]). The man trusting his riches cannot redeem himself or his brother. But the precious redemption of the soul is of God, through faith in Christ.
p: Silver and gold cannot deliver them in the day of wrath ([[Ezekiel 7:19]]). The stumbling block of their iniquity is their greed. Especially the Jews, who were and are respecters of persons in that regard. *Give me neither poverty nor riches* ([[Proverbs 30:8-9]]).

# from 17197, 5876
--- Rich yet nothing, poor yet rich
kicker: True riches
big: Proverbs 13:7 | mark: there is that maketh himself poor, yet hath great riches
p: God is not opposed to earthly riches. Job and Abraham and Solomon were incredibly wealthy in their day. It isn't the wealth we gain but what we do with it. It isn't the riches but where your treasure is.
p: The love of money is the root of all evil ([[1 Timothy 6:10]]). Be satisfied with what God blessed you with, even if it is subsistence, knowing that if you win souls, your riches in Heaven will be greater.

# from 17196, 17346, 17391
--- Low time preference
kicker: True riches
big: Proverbs 13:11 | mark: he that gathereth by labour shall increase
p: Low time preference is the tendency to delay immediate gratification in favor of future, long-term rewards. It reflects a patient, disciplined, and forward-thinking mindset, and it is a sign of wisdom.
p: *Be thou diligent to know the state of thy flocks* ([[Proverbs 27:23]]). But *he that hasteth to be rich hath an evil eye* ([[Proverbs 28:22]]). The “Prosperity Gospel” scam artists take as much advantage of this tendency in man as the street corner swindler.

# from 17269, 17340
--- The borrower is servant
kicker: True riches
big: Proverbs 22:7 | mark: the borrower is servant to the lender
p: We complain about the “unfair” reality of billionaires and the power of usury, while we rack up debt. We will contribute to the rich for things we don't need, while neglecting the brother at church who is struggling to pay for basic necessities.
p: *Boast not thyself of to morrow* ([[Proverbs 27:1]]). Now is the day of salvation ([[2 Corinthians 6:2]]). Be prudent and plan, without worry. Don't put off till tomorrow what can be done today.

# from 9522, 17491, 17528
--- Rich toward God
kicker: True riches
big: Luke 16:9 | mark: Make to yourselves friends of the mammon of unrighteousness
p: Make friends with your wealth. Spend it to others' benefit. Forgive them debts, that those you've helped may hear the Gospel. If we can't be just with unrighteous Mammon, how will we expect to be just with others' souls?
p: You can't take it with you ([[Ecclesiastes 5:15]]). But the works we do for the Lord are never in vain ([[1 Corinthians 15:58]]). All good things are of God, and it's okay to enjoy those things. They are a gift.

# from 17497
--- Say not the former days were better
kicker: True riches
big: Ecclesiastes 7:10 | mark: Say not thou, What is the cause that the former days were better than these?
p: Appeals to the “better days” of the past prey upon the inclination to see the former days as better, especially given the evil we see all around us. For we all feel like Lot at times.
p: There is no political solution to a spiritual problem. Revival is a continuous process. The need for revival is never greater than now. No matter when “now” is.
